# TODO - Ongoing compatibility assurance and product hardening

## Objective

Continuously find and fix product defects and improve usability, tests,
working Linux/Firefox personal delivery, and macOS/Safari/ChatGPT compatibility
without blocking
functional delivery. Keep this record open until explicitly closed by Alberto.

## Acceptance

**ONGOING -- NEVER AUTO-COMPLETE.** This final task is a continuously open
maintenance and product-improvement lane. Its acceptance criterion is **IT WORKS
better after each iteration**: find a real issue or useful improvement,
reproduce it where practical, add focused regression coverage, fix or improve
the implementation, and verify that existing behavior still works. Repeat this
cycle whenever development agents are authorized to work; the existence of this
TODO does not itself start a background agent or grant deployment authority.

Give provider-change detection unusually high and recurring maintenance
priority. Blooket is an external platform that can change between product
releases, silently invalidating previously working browser selectors, React
controls, routes, authentication states, transport payloads, timing assumptions,
and success/read-back signals. Allocate dedicated, reviewable commits to
compatibility probes, fixture drift detection, regressions and fixes, not just
new features.

Continuously evolve the test matrix even when the existing tests are green;
green fixtures from an older provider build are not proof the live integration
still works.

For every observed provider UI/API change or unexplained write/read failure:
record the observed browser/product build and affected contract, add a minimal
sanitized failing fixture or read-only smoke test, distinguish a real provider
change from an application regression, update the adapter only after review,
and validate ordinary, interrupted and ambiguous outcomes. Keep both positive
and fail-closed negative tests: changed or missing controls, hydration/order,
identity/session/navigation, overlays, question types/timing/media, and remote
persistence after reload. No credentials, captured private content, cookies,
or volatile provider action tokens belong in tracked fixtures.

Run `npm run provider:drift:check` for provider-facing source changes and
keep expanding its sanitized synthetic cases. Periodically perform
owner-authorized read-only browser compatibility checks against the actual
connected provider session. Keep opt-in and human stops for account
mutations, challenges, browser permissions and unfamiliar access states. A drift
signal must prevent unsafe writes or honestly mark verification inconclusive;
it must never trigger automatic selector guessing, security bypass, or replay
of ambiguous mutations.

Document evidence, failures and unresolved risks with each
compatibility-focused commit. This is ongoing intensive maintenance, not a
finite box to check or a requirement to block safe feature delivery.

Focus on bugs, missing tests, correctness, recovery, edge cases, performance,
behavior, EN/ES UX, accessibility, UI polish, Firefox/Safari/ChatGPT
compatibility, **theoretical macOS ARM64 compatibility assurance** from
official docs, contracts, portable tests and opt-in native CI, and reliable
Linux x64 user installations and native checks. Keep Mac-only concerns here;
never claim a real recipient-Mac
or native behavior was tested when it was only inferred.

**Manual closure gate:** do NOT mark this record completed, move it to
`docs/todo/completed/`, or remove it from `TODO.md` unless Alberto explicitly
instructs closing **this exact ongoing task**. Passing tests, fixing known bugs,
shipping an app, or completing the other TODOs does not satisfy that gate.
Remain active indefinitely until that explicit instruction, even with zero
currently known bugs. Never block functional delivery or safe releases solely
because this ongoing improvement queue remains open.

Keep provider challenges, consent, secret boundaries, honest verification, and
safe rollback intact. This task can discover and correct issues in previously
completed areas without reopening their milestones; never self-approve a
release, modify accounts without authorization, or weaken existing safeguards
just to obtain green tests.

## Compatibility coverage guidance

Use current official Apple, WebKit, GitHub Actions, and ChatGPT/MCP
documentation. Deterministic implementation, protocol tests, and native ARM64
CI are sufficient engineering evidence when manual interaction adds no distinct
fact.

The opt-in CI path is linear: general validation runs first on Linux. Only after
it passes does macos-arm run on macos-26, require ARM64, build the single
supported Mac package, compile the Safari WebExtension host, and exercise the
packaged localhost UI through Safari WebDriver.

Keep production Apple trust, signing/notarization, Gatekeeper, installed
Safari behavior, native Keychain and login-item behavior, filesystem
normalization, and ChatGPT-specific compatibility here. Also own Safari-only
editor acceptance such as native chooser/clipboard permission behavior,
eyedropper interaction, accessibility/responsive behavior, and animated preview
replay. Do not reopen earlier records merely to demand a recipient Mac, Safari,
or ChatGPT smoke test.

A CAPTCHA, unfamiliar login, organization choice, consent prompt, browser
permission, or other genuine human-validation boundary still requires the human
when it occurs. That is an execution checkpoint, not a standing platform
blocker.

## Implementation notes

Keep general CI portable and platform-neutral. The macOS ARM job owns only
platform-specific build, Safari conversion, native package behavior, and Safari
WebDriver smoke coverage; Release owns only release gating and publication.

The supported Mac product architecture is ARM64. Intel/x64 is not a package,
update target, CI target, or release asset. Long-term client behavior for the
completed personal-skill system, native settings/Keychain behavior, and
ChatGPT-specific OAuth registration/reauthorization behavior also live only in
this final record.

## Evidence

On 2026-10-21, red-to-green regressions found that the asynchronous visible
question-controls editor could continue filling React inputs after a native
`dialog[open]` or ARIA alert dialog appeared, despite the outer Add Question
action rejecting those overlays. The injected controls preflight, repeated
ownership checks and final read-only echo now stop on those human surfaces.
Tests confirm that a dialog appearing during type selection prevents further
form changes. No remote Save or authenticated provider edit was performed.

On 2026-10-21, additional provider-drift regressions showed that Add Question
opening, preparation, image selection and submission did not recognize visible
native `dialog[open]` and `role="alertdialog"` human stops. The independent
injected action now refuses them at admission and after form/file events,
without clicking Save or replacing a teacher-owned editor. A hidden former
prompt does not block a safe question opener. This is sanitized synthetic
coverage and does not assert a new authenticated remote mutation.

On 2026-10-21, provider-drift fixtures exposed that Create Set preparation,
submission and redirect verification did not stop for visible native
`dialog[open]` and ARIA `alertdialog` surfaces without explicit modal markup.
The independent injected Create Set stages now reject those prompts, including
when they appear after an earlier ownership claim or during form preparation.
The sanitized tests require zero Save clicks under a new human gate. These
are portable synthetic protections, not evidence of a live provider change.

On 2026-10-21, red-to-green broker regressions showed that nonfinite or
regressing time samples could dispatch a pending browser job, or accept an
expired saved-state reply. A second regression exposed a clock failure during
client pruning after an initially valid poll, which still granted a lease.

The broker now poisons its monotonic clock for the rest of that instance,
retiring queued jobs, declining dispatch and refusing stale completions rather
than ever replaying a potentially committed Save. A separate red-to-green
test showed that lease time could expire *during* response decoding after an
initially valid completion check. The broker now checks the time again after
decoding before accepting any read-back or Save reply. No actual provider
write was made: these are sanitized, local bridge tests.

A 2026-10-21 red-to-green authentication-host regression found that the
credential submission gate could accept a `NaN` or backward-moving deadline
sample after preparing the exact login form. The host now refuses invalid
or regressed clock samples for the entire explicit login attempt; no
credential-bearing script or Submit can be newly authorized after such a
failure. Synthetic tests contain only fictional credentials and do not
constitute a live sign-in or provider acceptance.

A separate 2026-10-21 red-to-green drift regression found five independently
injected question-read functions that still admitted clicks and positive
read observations behind visible native HTML dialogs and ARIA alertdialogs.
All now reject the visible human-owned overlay before enumerating, opening,
inspecting or closing a question panel. The sanitized suite proves no Save
is attempted; the disconnected browser session did not provide new live
provider DOM evidence.

A 2026-10-21 red-to-green provider-drift regression found that the eight
isolated capability helpers blocked visible ARIA alertdialogs but still
admitted a visible native `dialog[open]` without ARIA attributes. Their
self-contained human-stop checks now cover both selector classes before
opening, observing or closing read-owned controls; hidden dialogs do not
block legitimate reads. This is synthetic compatibility coverage, not
acceptance from a live provider build.

A 2026-10-21 native-dialog regression found that the content-free editor
compatibility probe could claim `shape-compatible` while a `dialog[open]`
required the teacher's attention. A saved-question image read could likewise
confirm a digest after an open dialog appeared during transfer. Both now
refuse those receipts, retaining positive behavior once the dialog is hidden.
This is synthetic fail-closed coverage of standard HTML, not new provider
acceptance or evidence of an actual changed Blooket DOM.

A 2026-10-21 red-to-green navigation regression covered native HTML
`dialog[open]`: an active browser-native modal need not set an explicit ARIA
role or attribute, and navigating away can discard a teacher decision. The
capability read-leave guard now stops for visible open dialogs, while hidden
or dismissed native dialogs do not block ordinary reads. This is portable
synthetic compatibility evidence, not a claim of observed provider DOM drift.

A subsequent 2026-10-21 concurrent suite exposed a different false negative
in the launcher tests: a deliberately non-draining stale-PID service exhausted
the test subprocess's eight-second timeout (`code: null`) instead of letting
the real launcher report its expected unsafe-stop refusal (`code: 1`).
The fixture now allows bounded scheduling overhead beyond the real five-second
closure observation, and still rejects a missing/incorrect exit code. It does
not relax the launcher's actual shutdown proof or kill an unknown service.

A 2026-10-21 synthetic image-read regression found that an owned question
media GET could still return a positive prepared-byte identity if a visible
`alertdialog` appeared before or during the async transfer. The page helper
now rechecks that human gate on each existing ownership observation and
refuses the digest, without revealing URLs or retrying Save. This is portable
fail-closed evidence, not a new Blooket upload or live receipt.

The same 2026-10-21 alertdialog drift scenario also fooled the independent
read-only question-editor compatibility probe into reporting `shape-compatible`
during a visible human decision prompt. It now reports `human-stop` until that
prompt is hidden, without reading answers or changing the browser page.
Sanitized fixtures, not a live Blooket DOM change, establish this regression.

A companion 2026-10-21 red-to-green drift regression found that a visible
`alertdialog` did not revoke the capability probe's owned Add Question/Audio
controls. All eight independent Chrome-injected capability functions now
refuse new clicks and read-owned Cancel after that human gate appears;
nonvisible alerts do not block ordinary hydration. Each function retains its
self-contained scripting boundary. This is portable provider-surface
hardening, not a new claim about current Blooket UI or remote persistence.

A 2026-10-21 sanitized provider-drift regression found that a visible
`role="alertdialog"` without `aria-modal` did not stop the native page-leave
guard. The read-only gate now refuses navigation during such a human prompt,
while preserving the existing visibility checks and hidden-alert behavior.
This is a portable fail-closed compatibility test, not proof of a changed
live provider DOM or a newly verified remote Save.

A previous concurrent `npm run validate` run reported an intermittent
`owned-launcher-service-did-not-drain` failure in the silent supervised
launcher integration test; the same test passed isolated. The test now uses
a monotonic eight-second drain observation and permits bounded scheduling
contention across the three child launches/two shutdowns. Its unchanged
assertions still require actual runtime/lock/lease disappearance and genuine
`--stop` success; no test cleanup grants permission to kill a foreign service.
This improves deterministic Linux regression assurance, not provider state.

On 2026-10-21, a red-to-green Studio publication regression showed that
getter-backed, inherited and extra progress fields could cross the UI
command boundary. A mutable progress callback could also retarget the
checkpoint and let verification run against a different set. The UI now
snapshots exact own data fields and reports independent copies, without
changing the canonical journal or repeating any Blooket Save. These are
portable synthetic tests, not new live provider acceptance.

On 2026-10-21, the separate autonomous image/typing quiz passed fresh canonical
verification at 15:56 and 16:00 UTC with three operations complete. Both
questions were saved by the product. Functional tasks blooket-11 and
blooket-12 moved to completed records; this evergreen task remains active.

Intermittent provider-read failure still needs compatibility work: one
registered-MCP verification failed between those successful CLI checks.
Preserve that distinction, the strict read gates and historical journals;
never infer a failed mutation or authorize Save replay from a failed read.

On 2026-10-20, a red-to-green browser-lifecycle regression found that a newly
created detached read tab could report Chrome's initial `about:blank` with
`status: complete` before its requested URL became visible. The shared reader
immediately classified that short transition as foreign navigation, even
though it owned the new tab.

Only freshly created capability/question readers now tolerate this specific
bounded pre-commit state; existing teacher tabs, foreign pending routes and
stuck blank pages continue to fail closed. Synthetic
Chrome tests cover successful commit, no page scripts before commitment, and
owned cleanup. This does not resolve or certify the last real replacement
preflight failure; a fresh connected-browser read is still required.


An opt-in Linux desktop shortcut is now packaged with the x64 executable.
Native Linux smoke ran the packaged `install-desktop-shortcut` against a
synthetic user home: first install succeeded, while the second refused to
replace the existing entry. Portable tests also refuse unsafe executable
paths and keep the menu separate from service start and user data. Real
machine-wide installation and personal menus were not modified.

A 2026-10-20 red-to-green host regression added an explicit per-launch
Firefox selection on Linux and macOS without changing the OS default browser.
`BLOOKET_BROWSER=firefox` uses an allowlisted native executable/argument
sequence and the existing reduced child environment; malformed preferences
cannot become executable paths or injected flags.

On 2026-10-20, the Firefox 158 browser was exercised with a temporary
compiled Firefox WebExtension in an isolated headless profile. Mozilla's
`web-ext lint --self-hosted --warnings-as-errors` found that the initial
Firefox 128 minimum did not support the declared data-collection fields.
The minimum is now Firefox 142, with zero linter errors or warnings.

Native `web-ext run` installed the extension and a synthetic loopback page
announced itself four times. A local refusal-only bridge recorded matching
Firefox extension origin and correct bearer authentication on all four
status requests, without pairing or opening a Blooket account tab.
This verifies native Firefox extension execution and transport bootstrap;
it does not establish live authenticated Blooket account acceptance.

On 2026-10-15, a content-free authorized Chrome read found one same-origin
HTTP 429 resource response on the provider Edit route during initial page
loading. This is not a Save receipt, nor proof that the later Save was
throttled. A dedicated isolated-world diagnostic now records a monotonic
timestamp before
a single Save click and only classifies HTTP 429 fetches to that exact same
Edit route after the attempt.

Earlier hydration 429s, unrelated set IDs, foreign initiators, replaced
documents and unowned latches are refused.
Synthetic provider-drift tests prove the positive and negative shapes; an
uncertain attempted write remains journaled without auto-retry.

The browser mutation host now separates temporarily missing document-origin
evidence after its own navigation from absent or malformed identity on a
teacher-selected existing tab. These cases cannot be polled into authority;
synthetic tests assert zero modal opens and zero Save attempts.

Dedicated read/navigation drift tests now cover a same-route document
replacement during Chrome's final `tabs.get`, temporary loss of question-card
controls after an owned Cancel, and persistent or malformed post-Cancel
results. They fail closed without untrusted adoption or another Save.
The live Chrome check reported compatible Edit selectors, not a new saved
question receipt; the user journals and unsaved editor were preserved.

A same-day read-only Chrome 155 structural check against the existing
Typing editor still found the expected numeric timer, visible prompt and
Save Question control. The current Blooket provider build was not identifiable
from this observation. The canonical authenticated session read succeeded but
My Sets and capability reads remained unconfirmed while a local unsaved editor
was present. The journal remained untouched; this compatibility signal alone
is not a remote publication receipt.

See
`docs/technical/provider-change-detection.md` for the scoped evidence.

On 2026-10-14, an explicit `provider:drift:check` command grouped the native
Blooket page and extension-host regressions. It passed 290 synthetic tests
before adding a separate read-only shape inspector for the question editor.
The inspector's fixture cases distinguish a valid Typing/Multiple Choice form
from renamed answer slots, missing selectors, changed time controls, malformed
